course: dictionary
lesson: 8542
title: How a scab protects a wound
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A scab is a hard, dry, dark crust that forms over a cut or graze. You can see it on the outside, but the healing happens underneath. «A cut heals because skin cells around it divide and make new cells that crawl across the wound, while a scab protects it from germs.»
explain: The sentence gives you clues. The scab sits on top, and new skin grows below it. So a scab works like a plaster that your body makes for itself. «The scab is the body's own plaster, so leave it alone.»
explain: A scab forms because blood clots and dries. «Platelets stick together at the cut and threads of a protein called fibrin form a net that traps blood cells into a clot, which dries into a scab.» When the new skin is ready, the scab falls off by itself.
fact: A scab is a hard, dry, dark crust that forms over a cut or graze.
fact: New skin grows under a scab while the wound heals.
fact: «Pick a scab» can let germs in and leave a scar.
fact: A scab falls off by itself once the skin underneath has healed.
fact: Long ago, scab was also an insult for a worker who kept working during a strike.
exercise: «The scab is the body's own plaster, so leave it alone.» What is a scab? | A piece of skin that is pulled off | *A hard, dry crust that forms over a cut while the skin heals | A soft, wet bandage from a hospital | A type of germ that gets into cuts
exercise: Why should you not pick a scab? | It makes the scab grow bigger | It is always painful to touch | It makes the blood clot faster | *It can let germs in and leave a scar
exercise: Where does the new skin grow while a cut heals? | *Underneath the scab | Above the scab, in the air | Inside the blood | Away from the cut, on another part of the body
test: «Threads of fibrin form a net that dries into a scab.» What dries to make a scab? | Fat from under the skin | A thin layer of dust | Sweat from the skin | *A clot of blood cells held in a net
test: When does a scab fall off? | After one day, every time | *When the skin underneath has healed | When you pull it off with your finger | When you have a bath
exam: What is a scab? | A soft, wet bandage from a hospital | A sore, open cut that is still bleeding | A worker who never stops working | *A hard, dry, dark crust that forms over a cut while new skin grows underneath
